{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implemented on branch fix/9ec0-reconciler-drain (commit 7af470bc4), committed locally, NOT pushed.\n\n## Verified against the tree (gerrit/main 36e986c188)\n\nRoot cause confirmed by reading: `app.py` lifespan awaits `queue.join()` only; `reconcile_once`\nawaits `review_and_vote` INLINE with `gerrit=`/`dedup=` injected, outside that queue. With an\nempty queue the join returns at once and the reconcile task is cancelled within\n`shutdown_cancel_seconds()` (10s).\n\nCORRECTION to this ticket's \"Suspected fix\": it proposes waiting on `voter.in_flight_reviews()`\n\"that 34cd added\". That symbol does NOT exist on gerrit/main — 34cd's work is on an unpushed\nlocal branch (db241b80ab), and `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` has no defer logic either. So the\ndeploy-side mitigation is not in the tree, and this fix had to be self-contained.\n\n## Shape chosen: cooperative stop + a gated, budget-shared wait\n\nRejected \"route reconciler reviews through the worker queue\": the queue is fire-and-forget, so\n`reconcile_once`'s `{scanned, reviewed}` return (asserted in 6 tests) becomes unobtainable; the\nworker does NOT inject gerrit/dedup so the shared-client reuse is lost; and with WORKER_COUNT=1\nbackfill would serialize behind webhook work, which today runs concurrently as a separate task.\n\nImplemented instead: `reconcile.request_stop()` makes the loop take no NEW candidate and return\nonce the in-flight review lands, so the reconcile task itself is awaitable as a drain. The\nlifespan requests the stop BEFORE either wait, drains the queue, then waits for the reconcile\ntask — gated on `review_in_flight()`.\n\n## Bounding\n\nBoth halves share ONE `shutdown_drain_seconds()` deadline. Independent windows would allow 2x1200s;\nwith the 60s store-lock budget the floor would be 2460s against a 1320s stop_grace_period, so\nsharing the deadline is what keeps the existing grace-period relationship true (floor stays 1260s).\n`asyncio.wait`, not `wait_for` — wait_for's timeout path cancels then awaits unboundedly, which\nre-broke the c2ba hang and was caught by the existing c2ba test.\n\nThe `review_in_flight()` gate exists because an unconditional wait made a reconciler parked in a\nblocking events-log fetch hold the FULL budget (a real regression: ~10s shutdown becomes minutes).\n\n## Tests\n\nRED proven before the fix, and each guard mutation-tested as load-bearing. Coverage is placed in\nthe DEFAULT tier as well as the lifespan, because CI's pytest lane installs only `[dev]` and every\n`pytest.importorskip(\"fastapi\")` test SKIPS there — 9 skips in this file, including the existing\nshutdown-drain and c2ba bounded-shutdown tests. Lifespan-only coverage would have been a skip in CI.\n"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785796040524725001,"uuid":"b008bf44-9767-45d2-8bb0-60c3cdb40ad7"}